    "# Skills: Reuse the Workflow, Not Just the Prompt\n",
    "\n",
    "A **prompt** gives instructions for one interaction. A **Skill** packages a repeatable workflow so the stable “how to do this” does not need to be rewritten every time. A Skill can contain instructions, examples, code, and supporting resources.\n",
    "\n",
    "For the repository workflow in this tutorial, a reusable Skill could preserve the stable method:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. inspect the issue and repository rules;\n",
    "2. restate scope and acceptance criteria;\n",
    "3. make a short plan;\n",
    "4. edit only allowed files;\n",
    "5. run targeted tests;\n",
    "6. inspect `git diff` and `git status`;\n",
    "7. stop before a consequential action such as merge or deploy.\n",
    "\n",
    "Task-specific facts—such as the issue number, exact files, current failure, or acceptance criteria—should still be supplied for the current task.\n",
    "\n",
    "A conceptual Skill folder could look like:\n",
    "\n",
    "```text\n",
    "repo-change-review/\n",
    "├── SKILL.md\n",
    "├── examples/\n",
    "└── scripts/\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "> **Human control still applies.** Reusing a Skill should make the workflow more consistent; it should **not** silently expand what the agent is allowed to read, change, publish, or approve."
   ]
  },
